# The build context is the repository root (see `render.yaml`'s `dockerContext`
# and the note at the top of `backend/Dockerfile`), so without this file every
# build tars up the whole working tree and hands it to the Docker daemon —
# including three workspaces' `node_modules`, a `.next` cache, and, if Postgres
# has ever been run from this repo, its data directory.
#
# Nothing here is COPY'd by the Dockerfile today. This is not what makes that
# true, and it is not the reason to have the file: the reason is that a build
# context containing secrets and gigabytes of other people's build output is a
# slow build and a wide one, and both get worse before anyone notices.

# Dependencies. Built for the host, not for alpine — a native module copied in
# from a Windows or macOS working tree would not load in the image anyway.
**/node_modules

# Build output. The image builds its own from source; a stale `dist` in the
# context is the one thing here that could quietly change what ships.
**/dist
**/.next
**/out
**/build
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Test and coverage output
**/coverage
**/.nyc_output
shared/mutation-report.json

# Secrets. Never in a build context: it is uploaded to the daemon, and on a
# hosted builder that means leaving this machine.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Version control and editors
.git
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local database volume, if Postgres has been run from this repo
database/.pgdata

# Logs and scratch
**/*.log
npm-debug.log*
logs

# Platform metadata for other hosts. The frontend ships to Vercel and is not
# part of this image; these are excluded for context size only.
.vercel
.render
